// Original educational content (English edition). Not ISACA text.
window.COBIT_CONTROLS_EN = window.COBIT_CONTROLS_EN || [];
window.COBIT_CONTROLS_EN.push(...
[
{"objective":"APO01","purpose":"The IT management framework brings policies, processes, roles, organizational structures and the tools that support them together into a single, coherent system. Without such a framework, controls remain dependent on individuals, accountabilities become blurred and management decisions are not consistently reflected in day-to-day operations. A well-designed framework provides the common foundation for all other COBIT objectives.","auditFocus":"The senior auditor challenges whether the framework exists only on paper, and whether policies, roles and committees actually operate in practice and are supported by decision records. Particular attention is paid to ownership, authority levels, policy exceptions and the traceability of the improvement cycle.","keyRisks":["Critical decisions left without an owner because roles and authorities are unclear","Outdated or unenforced policies providing false assurance","Access and classification decisions made arbitrarily because data and system ownership has not been defined"],"practices":[
{"code":"APO01.01","name":"Design the management system for enterprise I&T.","title":"Designing the IT management system to fit the organization’s needs","expectation":"An approved design document is expected that is derived from enterprise goals and shows which management objectives are prioritized and which components (processes, structures, policies, information flows) will support them. The design should take into account factors such as the risk profile, size of the organization, regulatory environment and sourcing model, and should be reviewed at intervals defined by the organization or upon significant change. The owner of the design and the approving authority should be clearly defined.","design":"Assess whether the design establishes a traceable link to enterprise goals and the risk profile, and whether areas excluded from scope are justified.","operating":"Compare review and update records for the period against significant organizational changes; for selected events, verify that the changes were reflected in the design.","evidence":["Approved IT management system design document or governance framework","Goals cascade or analysis showing prioritized management objectives","Meeting minutes and version history evidencing review of the design"],"findings":["The management system design is not documented; practices are shaped by the individual habits of each unit.","A design document exists but has not been updated following the latest organizational change and the adoption of a new cloud service model."],"iso":[],"clauses":["4.1","4.3","4.4"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO01.02","name":"Communicate management objectives, direction and decisions made.","title":"Communicating management objectives and decisions to stakeholders","expectation":"Senior management’s IT-related objectives, priorities and significant decisions should be communicated to relevant stakeholders through a planned communication approach. The content, target audience, channel and frequency of communication should be defined by the organization, and understanding of key messages should be monitored through means such as surveys, feedback or meeting attendance.","design":"Evaluate whether the communication plan defines target audiences and message owners, and whether it includes a mechanism to ensure that critical decisions reach the relevant teams.","operating":"List significant management decisions taken during the period; for a risk-based selection of decisions, inspect announcement records, distribution lists and evidence of feedback.","evidence":["IT communication plan or communication calendar","Announcements, e-mails or intranet publications containing management decisions","Attendance records and presentations from briefing meetings","Results of employee awareness surveys or feedback"],"findings":["IT prioritization decisions made at management level were not communicated in writing to operations teams.","Communication activities are carried out, but there is no measurement of whether the messages have been understood."],"iso":[],"clauses":["5.1","7.4"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO01.03","name":"Implement management processes (to support the achievement of governance and management objectives).","title":"Implementing management processes against target maturity levels","expectation":"Target capability (maturity) levels and an implementation sequence should be defined for the processes prioritized in the management system design. Each process should have a defined owner, inputs, outputs and metrics; the gap between current and target levels should be assessed at intervals defined by the organization and linked to an improvement plan.","design":"Inspect whether target levels are justified by business priorities and risk assessment, and whether an owner and metrics are defined for each process.","operating":"For a risk-based selection of processes from the process inventory, re-examine the maturity assessment results, the supporting evidence and the follow-up of improvement actions.","evidence":["Process inventory and list of process owners","Assessment report showing target and current maturity levels","Process improvement roadmap and action tracking log"],"findings":["Target maturity levels have been set for processes, but there is no rationale for why these levels were chosen.","Maturity assessments rely solely on self-declaration, and no evidence is collected to support the declarations."],"iso":[],"clauses":["4.4","9.1"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO01.04","name":"Define and implement the organizational structures.","title":"Establishing IT governance committees and organizational structures","expectation":"Structures such as the IT steering committee, architecture board or change advisory board should have documented terms of reference, membership, decision rights and meeting frequency as defined by the organization. Joint governance structures established with external service providers should also be in scope, and committee decisions should be recorded in a traceable manner.","design":"Evaluate whether committee terms of reference cover authority limits, quorum rules, reporting lines and conflict-of-interest management.","operating":"Compare planned and held meetings over the period; for selected meetings, verify quorum, decision records and closure of actions through the minutes.","evidence":["Committee terms of reference and membership lists","Meeting calendar and approved minutes","Action tracking list for committee decisions","Current organization chart"],"findings":["The IT steering committee did not hold some of its planned meetings during the period, and pending decisions were postponed.","Committee minutes record the decisions taken but do not specify an owner or target date."],"iso":["5.4"],"clauses":["5.1","5.3"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO01.05","name":"Establish roles and responsibilities.","title":"Defining IT roles, responsibilities and authority levels","expectation":"A responsibility matrix (e.g. RACI) showing the responsible, accountable, consulted and informed parties for critical IT-related activities, together with current job descriptions, should be in place. Approval limits and signing authorities should be documented, designed in line with the segregation of duties principle, and updated when roles change.","design":"Analyze whether the matrix leaves overlapping or unowned activities, and whether incompatible duties (e.g. development and promotion to production) are assigned to the same role.","operating":"List role changes and new assignments during the period; for selected samples, inspect the job description, authority approval and alignment of system access rights with the defined role.","evidence":["Approved RACI matrix or responsibility allocation table","Current job descriptions","Authority and approval limits directive","Segregation of duties conflict analysis"],"findings":["Job descriptions were prepared a long time ago and are not aligned with current roles.","The same individual holds both change development and production promotion rights, and no compensating control has been defined."],"iso":["5.2","5.3","5.4"],"clauses":["5.3"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO01.06","name":"Optimize the placement of the IT function.","title":"Determining the position of the IT function within the organization","expectation":"The reporting line of the IT function should be set through a deliberate decision that takes into account its strategic importance to the organization and the degree of operational dependency. The IT leader’s access to senior management, participation in budget and prioritization decisions, and the independence of oversight functions such as information security should have been assessed.","design":"Inspect whether the placement decision is based on a documented analysis of alternatives, and whether oversight functions are sufficiently independent from the areas they oversee.","operating":"Verify through minutes the participation of an IT representative in senior management meetings during the period, and examples where IT was involved in critical decisions.","evidence":["Current organization chart and reporting lines","Management decision or analysis supporting the placement decision","IT agenda items in senior management meeting minutes"],"findings":["The information security function reports to the IT infrastructure manager whom it is expected to oversee.","The IT leader is not invited to strategic planning meetings and is informed of decisions after the fact."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO01.07","name":"Define information (data) and system ownership.","title":"Defining information (data) and system ownership","expectation":"A business owner should be assigned for critical information assets and applications, and owners should be aware of their responsibilities for classification, access approval and periodic access review. Ownership information should be maintained in the inventory and updated upon staff departures or organizational changes.","design":"Evaluate whether the ownership definition is linked to the classification and access management processes, and whether a control exists to prevent assets from being left without an owner.","operating":"Compare the completeness of the application and data inventory against independent sources such as the CMDB or license records; for a risk-based selection of critical systems, verify that the owner is an active employee and has approved access reviews.","evidence":["Information asset and application inventory including owner details","Information classification policy and classification records","Access review outputs approved by data owners"],"findings":["For some critical applications in the inventory, the recorded owners are individuals who have left the organization.","Ownership has been assigned entirely to the IT department, and business units do not participate in access and classification decisions."],"iso":["5.9","5.12","5.13","5.15"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"access"},
{"code":"APO01.08","name":"Define target skills and competencies.","title":"Defining target skills and competencies","expectation":"The technical and managerial competencies required to achieve management objectives should be defined in a competency framework and mapped to roles. The framework should be updated at intervals defined by the organization in response to new technologies and changes in the business model.","design":"Evaluate whether competency definitions are measurable at role level and whether critical areas such as security, cloud and data are covered.","operating":"Inspect update records; for selected roles, verify that the defined competencies are actually used in job postings, training plans and performance evaluations.","evidence":["Role-based IT competency framework or competency matrix","Update and approval records for the competency framework","Samples of job postings or training plans linked to competencies"],"findings":["A competency matrix exists but does not cover emerging needs such as cloud and cybersecurity.","The defined competencies are not referenced in training planning or recruitment processes."],"iso":[],"clauses":["7.2"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO01.09","name":"Define and communicate policies and procedures.","title":"Defining and communicating policies and procedures and monitoring compliance","expectation":"IT policies and procedures should be documented within a document hierarchy with a clear owner and approving authority, and published so that they are accessible to employees. Documents should be reviewed at intervals defined by the organization or upon significant change; compliance should be monitored, exceptions should be approved and time-bound, and the consequences of violations should be defined.","design":"Evaluate whether the policy management process covers version control, exception management, compliance measurement and the consequences of violations.","operating":"Check the review dates of all documents in the policy inventory; for a risk-based selection of exceptions, inspect approval and expiry conditions as well as compliance monitoring results.","evidence":["Policy and procedure inventory including version and approval information","Policy exception records and approvals","Employee policy read-and-acknowledge records","Policy compliance monitoring reports"],"findings":["Some policies are past their review date, and the roles they describe do not match the current organization.","Policy exceptions are approved by e-mail, with no central register and no expiry date."],"iso":["5.1","5.36","5.37"],"clauses":["5.2","7.5"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO01.10","name":"Define and implement infrastructure, services and applications to support the governance and management system.","title":"Providing tools and infrastructure that support the management system","expectation":"Tools supporting governance activities (GRC platform, risk register, policy portal, architecture repository, service management tool) should be selected based on need, have a defined owner and be subject to access control. Responsibility for the accuracy and currency of the information in these tools should be assigned, and the adequacy of the tools should be reviewed at intervals defined by the organization.","design":"Evaluate whether the tools are configured to meet data integrity, access authorization and record retention requirements.","operating":"For selected samples, compare the completeness and currency of records in the tools against source documents; inspect the list of users with administrator rights on a read-only basis.","evidence":["List of governance tools with ownership information","Role and permission reports from the tools (read-only export)","Current record views extracted from the risk register or policy portal"],"findings":["The risk register is maintained in spreadsheets with no version control or change trail.","Some users with administrator rights in the GRC tool do not require this level of access for their job."],"iso":[],"clauses":["7.1","7.5"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO01.11","name":"Manage continual improvement of the I&T management system.","title":"Continually improving the IT management system","expectation":"The effectiveness of the management system should be assessed at intervals defined by the organization using inputs such as audit findings, incidents, performance measurements and stakeholder feedback. Improvement opportunities should be prioritized and converted into owned, dated actions, and their closure and impact should be verified.","design":"Check whether the improvement process consolidates inputs from different sources into a single register, and whether it includes prioritization criteria and verification of effectiveness.","operating":"List all improvement actions opened during the period; for selected closed actions, re-examine the root cause, the change implemented and the evidence of effectiveness.","evidence":["Continual improvement or action tracking register","Management review meeting minutes","Effectiveness verification evidence for closed actions"],"findings":["Actions relating to prior audit findings appear as closed, but there is no evidence supporting their closure.","Improvement suggestions are collected but are left pending without prioritization."],"iso":["5.35"],"clauses":["9.3","10.1","10.2"],"itgc":""}
]},
{"objective":"APO02","purpose":"The IT strategy determines which digital capabilities the organization will invest in, in what sequence and with what level of resources, in order to achieve its business goals. IT that is disconnected from strategy spends resources on low-priority work and conflicts with the expectations of business units. A clear roadmap also makes investment and risk decisions consistent.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether the strategy is traceably derived from the business strategy and whether the gap between the current and target state has been translated into a realistic roadmap. The auditor also seeks evidence that the strategy has been approved and communicated, and that its execution is measured and monitored.","keyRisks":["IT investments made in a fragmented way, disconnected from business priorities","Unachievable targets set because current capabilities have not been realistically assessed","Business units launching conflicting initiatives because stakeholders are unaware of the strategy"],"practices":[
{"code":"APO02.01","name":"Understand enterprise context and direction.","title":"Understanding the enterprise context and business direction","expectation":"Before the IT strategy is prepared, industry trends, regulatory expectations, competitive conditions and the organization’s digitalization goals should be analyzed. This analysis should be performed together with business units, explicitly reference the enterprise strategy and be updated in each strategic planning cycle.","design":"Evaluate whether the context analysis covers key inputs such as the business strategy, regulatory environment and risk appetite, and whether it ensures the participation of business representatives.","operating":"Inspect the analysis documents, workshop records and stakeholder interview notes used in the latest planning cycle to verify that the analysis was actually performed and served as an input to the strategy.","evidence":["Enterprise strategy document and references to it in the IT strategy","Outputs of environmental and context analysis (e.g. PESTEL, SWOT)","Attendance and note records from strategy workshops held with business units"],"findings":["The IT strategy is based on assumptions that predate the update of the enterprise strategy.","The context analysis was performed by the IT team alone, without input from business units."],"iso":[],"clauses":["4.1","4.2"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO02.02","name":"Assess current capabilities, performance and digital maturity of the enterprise.","title":"Assessing current IT capabilities, performance and digital maturity","expectation":"The performance of current IT services, in-house and outsourced capabilities, technology assets and the level of digital maturity should be assessed on the basis of objective data. The assessment should include inputs such as service level reports, user satisfaction, technical debt and supplier performance, and should be recorded as a baseline.","design":"Inspect whether the assessment method uses defined criteria and whether results are based on measured data rather than assumptions.","operating":"Re-verify selected observations in the assessment report by comparing them against source data (service level reports, inventory, survey results).","evidence":["Current-state or digital maturity assessment report","Service performance and user satisfaction data","Application and infrastructure lifecycle (technical debt) analysis"],"findings":["The current-state assessment relies solely on management opinions and does not use measured performance data.","Outsourced services were not included in the scope of the assessment."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO02.03","name":"Define target digital capabilities.","title":"Defining target digital capabilities","expectation":"The IT products, services and capabilities to be achieved should be defined as the target state, driven by business direction. Targets should be measurable, take reference frameworks and industry practices into account, and address security and compliance requirements as part of the design.","design":"Evaluate whether target capabilities are linked to business goals and whether they include measurable success criteria as well as security and compliance requirements.","operating":"For selected target capabilities, trace the business owner’s approval, the success metrics and their consistent use in subsequent planning documents.","evidence":["Target-state capability map","Traceability matrix mapping targets to business goals","Management decision evidencing approval of the target state"],"findings":["The target state is defined in general terms and does not include measurable success criteria.","Information security and continuity requirements were not considered when defining target capabilities."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO02.04","name":"Conduct a gap analysis.","title":"Analyzing the gap between the current and target state","expectation":"Gaps between the current and target state should be identified across process, technology, people and supplier dimensions, and high-level changes to the enterprise architecture should be defined. For each gap, the impact, dependencies and an indicative resource requirement should be stated so that it is ready for prioritization.","design":"Inspect whether the gap analysis covers all dimensions and whether gaps contain sufficient information (impact, risk, cost) for prioritization.","operating":"Check whether selected items in the gap analysis are reflected in the roadmap; for those that are not, look for a documented decision to defer.","evidence":["Gap analysis report or table","Impact and priority assessment of gaps","Mapping of the gap analysis to the roadmap"],"findings":["The gap analysis addresses only the technology dimension and does not cover competency and process gaps.","Some critical gaps identified are not reflected in the roadmap, and no decision to defer them has been recorded."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO02.05","name":"Define the strategic plan and road map.","title":"Developing the digital strategy and roadmap","expectation":"The IT and digital strategy and roadmap, based on the gap analysis, should set out initiatives, sequencing, dependencies, owners, resource requirements and measurable targets. The strategy should be prepared with the involvement of relevant stakeholders, approved by the competent authority, and progress in execution should be reported at intervals defined by the organization.","design":"Evaluate whether the roadmap links initiatives to business goals, defines metrics and owners, and has gone through a formal approval process.","operating":"Compare progress reports for the period against the roadmap; for selected initiatives, verify that deviations were reported and that management took decisions.","evidence":["Approved IT or digital strategy document","Roadmap and list of initiatives","Strategy progress reports and management review minutes","Definitions of strategy performance indicators"],"findings":["The strategic plan has been approved, but progress in its execution has not been reported to any management body.","Owners and resource estimates for the initiatives in the roadmap have not been defined."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO02.06","name":"Communicate the I&T strategy and direction.","title":"Communicating the IT strategy and direction to stakeholders","expectation":"The approved IT strategy should be summarized at a level appropriate to each audience and communicated to business units, IT teams and, where necessary, critical suppliers. The scope and channels of communication should be defined by the organization, and confidential strategic information should be shared in accordance with classification rules.","design":"Inspect whether the communication approach takes into account different target audiences and information confidentiality levels.","operating":"Inspect announcements, presentations and distribution records made after the strategy was approved; corroborate awareness of the strategy through interviews with selected teams.","evidence":["Strategy announcement materials and presentations","Distribution lists or intranet publication records","Attendance records for briefing sessions"],"findings":["The strategy has been shared only with senior management, and the teams responsible for execution are unaware of the priorities.","A strategic plan classified as confidential has been shared in a common folder without access restrictions."],"iso":[],"clauses":["7.4"],"itgc":""}
]},
{"objective":"APO03","purpose":"Enterprise architecture shows how the business process, data, application and technology layers are interconnected and ensures that changes are made consistently with this whole. Without architecture governance, duplicate systems, integration complexity and uncontrolled technical debt accumulate. Common principles and standards help ensure that security and reusability are built in at the design stage.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether architecture principles and standards are documented and whether project compliance with these standards is actually checked through an architecture review. The auditor also evaluates, based on evidence, the currency of the architecture repository and the management of exceptions for non-standard solutions.","keyRisks":["Integration and security weaknesses arising from projects being deployed without adherence to architecture standards","Change impacts being incorrectly assessed because architecture documentation is out of date","Increased cost and complexity due to duplicate applications and technologies"],"practices":[
{"code":"APO03.01","name":"Develop the enterprise architecture vision.","title":"Developing the enterprise architecture vision","expectation":"An approved architecture vision document should exist that covers the business, data, application and technology layers and describes the current and target architecture at a high level. The vision should define scope, architecture principles, stakeholders and expected business benefits, and be consistent with the IT strategy.","design":"Evaluate whether the vision is linked to the IT strategy and business goals and whether it contains actionable architecture principles (e.g. reuse, secure by design).","operating":"Inspect approval and update records for the vision; for selected project documents, check whether they reference the principles in the vision.","evidence":["Approved architecture vision document","List of architecture principles","Mapping showing the relationship between the vision and the IT strategy"],"findings":["The architecture vision addresses only the technology layer and does not include the data and business process dimensions.","Architecture principles have been defined but have not been approved by management or communicated to projects."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO03.02","name":"Define reference architecture.","title":"Defining the reference architecture and keeping it current","expectation":"The current and target architecture should be maintained in an architecture repository at the level of business capabilities, data models, application portfolio, integrations and technology standards. Approved technology standards and security architecture principles should form part of the reference architecture, and the repository should be updated as changes occur.","design":"Inspect whether the reference architecture includes the approved technology list, security principles and data flows, and whether responsibility for updates has been assigned.","operating":"For selected samples, compare records in the architecture repository against the production inventory and recently deployed systems to verify currency.","evidence":["Views extracted from the architecture repository or modeling tool","List of approved technology standards","Application and integration map"],"findings":["The application map in the architecture repository does not include systems deployed in the recent period.","Some technologies no longer supported by the vendor are still included in the approved standards list."],"iso":["5.9","8.27"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO03.03","name":"Select opportunities and solutions.","title":"Selecting architecture opportunities and solution options","expectation":"Gaps between the current and target architecture should be assessed from both business and technical perspectives and grouped into meaningful work packages and projects. The comparison of solution options should be documented against cost, risk, reuse and architecture alignment criteria, and the prioritization decision should be taken by the competent authority.","design":"Evaluate whether solution selection criteria are defined and whether architecture alignment and security risks are among the decision criteria.","operating":"For solutions selected during the period, re-examine the assessment of alternatives, the scoring against criteria and the approval records.","evidence":["Comparative analyses of solution options","Architecture board decision records","Prioritized list of work packages or projects"],"findings":["The solution was selected on the basis of a single vendor proposal, and alternatives were not documented.","A new application performing the same function was purchased without the existing enterprise solution being evaluated."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO03.04","name":"Define architecture implementation.","title":"Developing the architecture implementation and transition plan","expectation":"An actionable plan for transitioning to the target architecture should exist, aligned with the program and project portfolio and showing dependencies and transition stages. The plan should cover interim solutions during the transition, systems to be retired, data migration and security requirements.","design":"Evaluate whether the transition plan is consistent with portfolio plans and whether it addresses system retirement, data migration and security.","operating":"Compare planned transition steps against actual progress; for selected retirements, inspect data archiving and access removal records.","evidence":["Architecture transition plan or roadmap","System retirement plans and completion records","Mapping of the transition plan to portfolio plans"],"findings":["Migration to the new system has been completed, but because the legacy system was not decommissioned, two parallel data sources remain in use.","The transition plan does not include data migration validation or security requirements."],"iso":["5.8"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO03.05","name":"Provide enterprise architecture services.","title":"Providing enterprise architecture services and guidance to projects","expectation":"The architecture team should provide guidance to projects, subject critical design decisions to an architecture review process and issue justified, approved and time-bound exception decisions for non-standard solutions. Architecture compliance should be checked using triggers defined by the organization (e.g. project stage gates).","design":"Evaluate whether the architecture review is defined as a mandatory checkpoint in the project lifecycle and whether the exception process includes formal risk acceptance.","operating":"Obtain the complete list of projects for the period; for a risk-based selection of projects, inspect the architecture review record, the closure of findings and exception approvals.","evidence":["Architecture review checklist and completed samples","Architecture exception register and approvals","Project stage gate records"],"findings":["Some projects were put into production without an architecture review.","Architecture exceptions are granted without an expiry date and are not subsequently followed up."],"iso":["5.8","8.27"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"}
]},
{"objective":"APO04","purpose":"Innovation management ensures that new technologies and ideas are explored and tested in a controlled manner and that those capable of generating value are adopted by the organization. Without a structured approach, either opportunities are missed or uncontrolled experiments create security and compliance risks. From the auditor’s perspective, what matters is that the resources used for innovation and the risks arising from it are visible.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether technology monitoring and idea evaluation activities are carried out against defined criteria and whether proof-of-concept (PoC) work is performed in an environment controlled from a data protection and security perspective. The auditor also considers whether successful experiments are properly transferred into the regular investment, architecture and change processes.","keyRisks":["Real personal or confidential data used without protection in uncontrolled experiments and pilots","New technologies adopted without organizational approval creating shadow IT","Technological developments that are competitively critical not being identified in time"],"practices":[
{"code":"APO04.01","name":"Create an environment conducive to innovation.","title":"Creating an environment that supports innovation","expectation":"Supporting elements such as an idea submission channel, an evaluation mechanism and time or budget dedicated to innovation should be defined within the organization. The recognition and reward approach should be documented, and it should be possible to trace where ideas originated and how they were concluded.","design":"Evaluate whether the idea collection and evaluation mechanism has transparent criteria and whether it addresses intellectual property and confidentiality.","operating":"Inspect idea records for the period; for selected ideas, verify the evaluation outcome, the feedback provided to the submitter and the reward records.","evidence":["Innovation policy or program definition","Idea management platform records","Records of budget or resources allocated to innovation"],"findings":["Suggestions from employees are recorded, but the evaluation outcome is not communicated to the submitter.","No dedicated resources or owner exist for innovation activities."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO04.02","name":"Maintain an understanding of the enterprise environment.","title":"Monitoring the organization’s business environment and needs","expectation":"The team or person responsible for innovation should understand business units’ problems and strategic priorities through regular contact and translate them into the innovation agenda. These interactions should take place on a schedule defined by the organization and their outcomes should be recorded.","design":"Evaluate whether a mechanism for interaction with business units (e.g. needs workshops, business relationship managers) is defined and whether its outputs are linked to the innovation agenda.","operating":"Trace meeting records with selected business units and the innovation agenda items arising from those meetings.","evidence":["Records of needs meetings with business units","Innovation agenda or list of needs","Business relationship manager role description"],"findings":["The innovation agenda is largely based on technology vendors’ proposals, and business needs are not collected systematically.","Discussions with business units are not recorded and therefore cannot be followed up."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO04.03","name":"Monitor and scan the technology environment.","title":"Monitoring and scanning the technology environment","expectation":"A technology watch process should be in place that systematically monitors new and emerging technologies, industry trends and lifecycle changes in the technologies in use. Sources, owners and reporting frequency should be defined by the organization, and findings should be communicated to the relevant decision-makers.","design":"Inspect whether the monitoring process has defined sources, evaluation criteria and a reporting line.","operating":"Inspect technology watch reports produced during the period; trace whether selected observations served as inputs to architecture or strategy decisions.","evidence":["Technology radar or watch reports","Definition of technology watch responsibilities and sources","Minutes of meetings at which the reports were presented"],"findings":["Technology monitoring depends on individual initiative and does not produce regular output.","Information that vendor support for a technology in use would end did not reach decision-makers in time."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO04.04","name":"Assess the potential of emerging technologies and innovative ideas.","title":"Assessing the potential of emerging technologies and ideas","expectation":"Promising technologies and ideas should be assessed in terms of business benefit, cost, risk and feasibility and, where necessary, tested through a limited-scope proof of concept (PoC). PoCs should be conducted with an approved scope, success criteria and duration; where real data is to be used, protective measures such as masking or anonymization should be defined in advance.","design":"Evaluate whether the PoC approval process includes success criteria, a security assessment and data usage rules.","operating":"List all PoCs for the period; for a risk-based selection, inspect the approval, type of data used, security assessment, post-trial data deletion and outcome report.","evidence":["PoC request and approval forms","PoC outcome and evaluation reports","Security and data protection assessment of the data used in PoCs"],"findings":["Unmasked customer data was used in a cloud-based PoC, and its deletion after the trial has not been verified.","PoCs were started without defined success criteria, so outcomes could not be evaluated objectively."],"iso":["5.34","8.33"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO04.05","name":"Recommend appropriate further initiatives.","title":"Recommending further initiatives for successful trials","expectation":"After PoC results have been evaluated, initiatives recommended to proceed should be transferred to the investment and portfolio management process together with a draft business case. Recommendations should clearly show stakeholder support and the resources required, and closure decisions for trials that are not continued should also be recorded.","design":"Evaluate whether a checkpoint exists to prevent the transition from PoC to permanent use from bypassing the regular portfolio, architecture and change processes.","operating":"Trace the outcome of PoCs completed during the period; for selected samples, inspect the recommendation document, the decision record and evidence of transfer to the portfolio.","evidence":["Initiative recommendation documents and draft business cases","Innovation or portfolio board decision records","Closure records for terminated PoCs"],"findings":["A pilot moved into permanent use without a formal investment decision.","For PoCs that were not continued, there is no closure decision or record that the resources were released."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO04.06","name":"Monitor the implementation and use of innovation.","title":"Monitoring the implementation and use of innovations","expectation":"Adopted innovations should be monitored throughout their lifecycle after deployment in terms of the extent to which they deliver the expected benefits, integration issues and emerging risks. Results and lessons learned should feed into future innovation decisions.","design":"Inspect whether benefit metrics, monitoring responsibility and risk assessment updates are defined for deployed innovations.","operating":"For selected innovations, inspect benefit monitoring reports, usage data and lessons learned records throughout the period.","evidence":["Benefit and usage monitoring reports by innovation","Lessons learned records","Updated risk assessments for adopted innovations"],"findings":["The usage rate and benefits delivered by newly deployed solutions are not measured.","Because lessons learned were not recorded, similar mistakes were repeated in subsequent trials."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""}
]},
{"objective":"APO05","purpose":"Portfolio management ensures that limited resources are directed to the IT programs, services and assets that will generate the most value. Without portfolio visibility, investment decisions are driven by personal priorities and failing initiatives cannot be stopped in time. Well-functioning portfolio management is the bridge between strategy and investment.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether investments are selected against defined and consistent criteria and whether the benefits in approved business cases are subsequently measured. The completeness of the portfolio inventory and whether stop or redirect decisions are taken for underperforming initiatives are also areas of focus.","keyRisks":["Investments approved without a business case or prioritization","Initiatives that do not generate value being continued because expected benefits are not measured","Resource conflicts and unrecorded spending going unnoticed because the portfolio inventory is incomplete"],"practices":[
{"code":"APO05.01","name":"Determine the availability and sources of funds.","title":"Determining funding sources and available funds","expectation":"Funding sources available for IT investments (internal budget, project budget, financing, subscription models) and their effect on expected returns should be determined together with the finance function. The amount of available funds should be clarified and approved before portfolio planning.","design":"Inspect whether the funding determination process is carried out jointly with the finance function and whether it assesses the cost implications of different financing options.","operating":"Inspect funding determination records and finance approvals for the latest planning cycle; compare the amount allocated to the portfolio with actual allocations.","evidence":["IT investment funding plan and finance approval","Analyses of financing options","Management decision for the portfolio planning period"],"findings":["Portfolio planning was performed before the amount of available funds was clarified, and some approved initiatives could not be funded.","The financing decision was made without comparing subscription and capital expenditure options."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO05.02","name":"Evaluate and select programs to fund.","title":"Evaluating and selecting programs to fund","expectation":"Program proposals should be submitted with business cases covering strategic alignment, expected benefits, cost, risk and resource requirements, and prioritized by scoring against consistent criteria defined by the organization. The selection decision should be taken by the competent authority, and rejected or deferred proposals should also be recorded with their rationale.","design":"Evaluate whether prioritization criteria are documented, weighted and designed to be applied consistently to all proposals.","operating":"List all investment proposals for the period; for a risk-based selection of approved and rejected proposals, re-examine the business case, scoring and approval authority.","evidence":["Business case documents","Prioritization criteria and scoring table","Investment board decision records","Approval authority matrix"],"findings":["Some projects were added to the portfolio directly on senior management instruction without a business case being prepared.","Although prioritization criteria are defined, no scoring was performed and the basis for decisions was not documented."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO05.03","name":"Monitor, optimize and report on investment portfolio performance.","title":"Monitoring and reporting investment portfolio performance","expectation":"The cost, schedule, scope and benefit performance of programs in the portfolio should be monitored and reported to management at intervals defined by the organization. For initiatives that deviate from targets or whose business case is no longer valid, decisions should be taken to reprioritize, change scope or stop.","design":"Evaluate whether reporting includes deviation thresholds and a decision-making mechanism, and whether reports are based on verifiable data.","operating":"Inspect portfolio reports for the period; for selected initiatives with deviations, compare reported data against project records and verify that management decisions were taken and implemented.","evidence":["Portfolio performance reports or dashboards","Deviation thresholds and escalation rules","Minutes relating to stop or redirect decisions"],"findings":["Portfolio reports show all projects as “green”, whereas project records contain significant schedule deviations.","No stop assessment was performed for a program whose business case is no longer valid."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO05.04","name":"Maintain portfolios.","title":"Keeping program, service and asset portfolios up to date","expectation":"Current and interlinked portfolio records should be maintained for investment programs and projects, IT products and services, and IT assets. Additions, changes and removals to the portfolio should go through a defined approval process, and records should be reviewed for completeness at intervals defined by the organization.","design":"Inspect whether portfolio records are maintained in a single authoritative source and whether additions and removals are subject to approval.","operating":"Test completeness by comparing portfolio records against independent sources such as finance, procurement and the asset inventory; inspect approval records for selected changes.","evidence":["Project and program portfolio list","IT service catalog","IT asset inventory","Portfolio change approval records"],"findings":["Some IT expenditures seen in procurement records are not linked to any portfolio record.","Services that are no longer offered continue to appear in the service catalog."],"iso":["5.9"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO05.05","name":"Manage benefits achievement.","title":"Managing the realization of benefits","expectation":"For benefits committed in approved business cases, an owner, metric, baseline value and target date should be defined, and realization should continue to be monitored after deployment. Where benefits are not realized, the root cause should be analyzed and corrective action taken, or the business case updated.","design":"Evaluate whether benefit tracking is designed to be handed over to the business owner rather than ending at project closure.","operating":"List the investments whose benefits should be measured during the period; for selected investments, recalculate the reported benefit values by comparing them against source business data.","evidence":["Benefits register","Post-implementation review reports","Source business data used for benefit measurement"],"findings":["Benefits included in approved business cases have never been measured after project closure.","Reported benefit amounts are based on unsupported estimates and are not validated against actual business data."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""}
]},
{"objective":"APO06","purpose":"IT budget and cost management ensures that IT spending is planned, monitored and correctly allocated to services in a transparent manner. When costs are not visible, discussions about value become subjective and budget overruns are identified late. Accurate cost information is also the basis for investment and resource prioritization decisions.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether the IT budget is based on portfolio priorities and whether actual costs are regularly compared against the budget with deviations explained. The transparency of the cost allocation model and the reconciliation of IT records with the finance system are also priority areas.","keyRisks":["Budget deviations not identified in time, leading to uncontrolled spending","Service costs and profitability being misjudged due to incorrect cost allocation","Reporting errors because IT costs are not reconciled with finance records"],"practices":[
{"code":"APO06.01","name":"Manage finance and accounting.","title":"Establishing IT finance and accounting management","expectation":"All IT-related costs, investments and depreciation should be classified and recorded using a method consistent with the organization’s accounting system and chart of accounts. An owner for IT financial management should be designated, and IT records should be reconciled with finance records at intervals defined by the organization.","design":"Evaluate whether the IT cost classification (operating versus capital expenditure distinction, cost centers) is documented and whether a reconciliation control is defined.","operating":"Inspect reconciliations for the period; for selected IT expenditures, re-verify through supporting documents that they were recorded to the correct cost center and account class.","evidence":["IT cost classification guideline or chart of accounts mapping","Reconciliations between IT and finance records","Capitalization and depreciation records"],"findings":["Some software subscription expenditures were recorded as capital expenditure rather than operating expense without justification.","Differences between IT expenditure records and the general ledger were left unexplained."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO06.02","name":"Prioritize resource allocation.","title":"Prioritizing resource allocation","expectation":"A decision process setting out the criteria by which IT resources (budget, staff, infrastructure) are allocated should be defined, together with rules governing IT spending that business units may undertake on their own initiative. Allocation decisions should be consistent with portfolio priorities and recorded with their rationale.","design":"Evaluate whether allocation rules include spending limits by business unit and controls to prevent unapproved IT acquisitions.","operating":"Inspect allocation decisions and IT-related spending by business units during the period to verify that the rules were applied and that limit overruns were approved.","evidence":["Resource allocation rules and authority limits","Management minutes for allocation decisions","IT spending reports by business unit"],"findings":["Business units purchase cloud service subscriptions without IT approval.","Resource allocation decisions are made verbally without their rationale being recorded."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO06.03","name":"Create and maintain budgets.","title":"Preparing and maintaining the IT budget","expectation":"The IT budget should be prepared based on the priorities in portfolio and service plans, clearly showing operating and capital items, assumptions and owners. The budget should be approved by the competent authority, and revisions during the period should also go through a defined approval process.","design":"Inspect whether the budget preparation process is linked to portfolio decisions and whether it ensures that revisions are approved in line with the authority matrix.","operating":"Compare the approved budget with the budget loaded into the finance system; list all revisions during the period and inspect the approval and rationale for selected revisions.","evidence":["Approved IT budget and budget assumptions","Budget revision requests and approvals","Mapping of budget items to the portfolio list"],"findings":["Budget items are not linked to portfolio initiatives and were prepared by uplifting prior-period spending.","Some in-period budget revisions were not approved at the level specified in the authority matrix."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO06.04","name":"Model and allocate costs.","title":"Building the cost model and allocating costs","expectation":"IT costs should be allocated to services and business units through a transparent cost model based on service definitions. Allocation keys should be documented, agreed with business units and reviewed against actual consumption data at intervals defined by the organization.","design":"Evaluate whether allocation keys are based on measurable consumption data and whether a mechanism exists for business units to challenge allocations.","operating":"Recalculate allocations for selected periods using resource consumption data and the approved allocation keys.","evidence":["IT cost model and allocation key documentation","Allocation calculation files","Chargeback reports sent to business units"],"findings":["Costs are allocated using fixed percentages rather than actual consumption, and these percentages have not been updated for a long time.","Allocation calculations are performed in manually prepared spreadsheets, and differences were identified on recalculation."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO06.05","name":"Manage costs.","title":"Monitoring and managing costs against budget","expectation":"Actual IT costs should be compared against the budget at intervals defined by the organization; significant deviations should be identified against defined thresholds, analyzed and communicated to the responsible owner. Deviations should be explained, corrective actions taken and forecasts updated where necessary, and cost-saving opportunities should also be monitored.","design":"Evaluate whether the cost monitoring process includes deviation thresholds, a requirement for explanations and escalation rules.","operating":"Inspect all budget-versus-actual reports for the period; for deviations exceeding the threshold, verify that explanations and actions were provided on time, and compare selected amounts with the finance system.","evidence":["Budget-versus-actual comparison reports","Variance analyses and explanations","Cost forecast updates","Minutes of management meetings at which cost reports were discussed"],"findings":["No explanation was requested and no action was taken for budget deviations exceeding the threshold.","Actual costs reported to management do not agree with the amounts in the finance system."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""}
]},
{"objective":"APO07","purpose":"The quality and security of IT services depend largely on having people with the right competencies in the right roles. Staff shortages, over-reliance on key individuals and inadequate training create conditions for errors, outages and security incidents. Subjecting outsourced and contract staff to the same rules is also necessary to manage people-related risk as a whole.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether key-person dependencies are identified and reduced, whether competency and training needs are monitored by role, and whether onboarding and offboarding processes cover security requirements. Policy acknowledgment, confidentiality undertakings and removal of access at contract end for contract staff are specifically sampled; evidence containing personal data is handled with due regard for confidentiality.","keyRisks":["Service continuity being jeopardized because critical knowledge and competency depend on a single individual","Inadequately trained staff causing errors or security breaches","Contract staff working outside policy or retaining access after their contract ends"],"practices":[
{"code":"APO07.01","name":"Acquire and maintain adequate and appropriate staffing.","title":"Ensuring adequate and appropriate IT staffing","expectation":"IT staffing needs (in-house and outsourced) should be assessed at intervals defined by the organization and upon significant organizational or technological change. Recruitment should be based on role requirements; pre-employment screening should be proportionate to applicable legislation and the sensitivity of the role, and employment contracts should include security and confidentiality obligations.","design":"Evaluate whether workforce planning is based on workload and risk assessment, and whether the recruitment process makes screening and security undertaking steps mandatory.","operating":"Obtain the complete list of IT staff hired during the period from the HR system; for a risk-based selection, inspect the screening record, signed contract and confidentiality undertaking, with personal data masked.","evidence":["IT workforce plan and needs analysis","Recruitment records and role requirement definitions","Screening completion records (with personal details masked)","Samples of signed employment contracts and confidentiality undertakings"],"findings":["For some individuals hired into highly privileged IT roles, there is no record that the screening step was completed.","Staffing needs were not reassessed following the recent infrastructure transformation, resulting in persistent overtime in critical teams."],"iso":["6.1","6.2"],"clauses":["7.1"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO07.02","name":"Identify key IT personnel.","title":"Identifying key IT personnel and reducing key-person dependency","expectation":"Roles that create dependency on a single individual for critical systems and processes should be identified, and backup staff, succession plans and documented work instructions should exist for these roles. Measures such as job rotation or mandatory uninterrupted leave should be applied as defined by the organization.","design":"Evaluate whether the key personnel analysis is linked to critical systems and whether backup and knowledge transfer measures are defined for each key role.","operating":"For selected key roles, verify through inspection and interviews that the backup is competent (e.g. actually performed the role during the period) and that work instructions are current.","evidence":["Key personnel and single-person dependency analysis","Succession and backup plans","Current work instructions for critical processes","Knowledge transfer or shadowing records"],"findings":["Maintenance of a critical application is known by only one specialist, and there is no backup staff or current documentation.","A key personnel list has been prepared, but whether backups are competent to take over the role has never been tested."],"iso":["5.37"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO07.03","name":"Maintain the skills and competencies of personnel.","title":"Maintaining staff skills and competencies","expectation":"Whether staff have the competencies required for their roles should be assessed at intervals defined by the organization, and training, certification or mentoring should be planned to address gaps. Information security awareness training should be planned for all IT staff and role-specific security training for staff with privileged access, with completion monitored.","design":"Evaluate whether the training plan is based on competency gaps and whether escalation is defined for cases where mandatory training is not completed.","operating":"Obtain from the learning management system the complete list of mandatory training assignments made during the period; inspect completion rates and the training records of selected privileged users.","evidence":["Competency assessment results","Periodic training plan","Completion reports from the learning management system","Certificate and attendance records"],"findings":["There is no follow-up or escalation for staff who have not completed mandatory security awareness training.","The training plan is not based on competency assessment and is built around individual requests."],"iso":["6.3"],"clauses":["7.2","7.3"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO07.04","name":"Assess and recognize/reward employee job performance.","title":"Evaluating and rewarding staff performance","expectation":"The performance of IT staff should be evaluated in a timely manner against individual objectives derived from enterprise goals and role responsibilities (including security and compliance responsibilities). Results should be linked to development plans, recognition and rewards, and a defined improvement process should exist for underperformance.","design":"Inspect whether individual objectives are linked to IT goals and whether control responsibilities are included among the evaluation criteria.","operating":"Check the completion status for all IT staff in the evaluation cycle; for selected evaluations, inspect objective documents and development plans, handling personal data confidentially.","evidence":["Performance management policy","Evaluation completion report","Anonymized samples of scorecards and development plans"],"findings":["Performance evaluations were not carried out for some IT staff even though the evaluation period has closed.","Individual objectives are not linked to the IT strategy or to control responsibilities."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO07.05","name":"Plan and track the usage of IT and business human resources.","title":"Planning and tracking the use of IT and business human resources","expectation":"Current and future demand for business and IT staff performing IT-related tasks should be monitored through capacity planning. Resource shortfalls and surpluses should be identified, feed into hiring, redeployment or outsourcing decisions, and be consistent with portfolio decisions.","design":"Evaluate whether resource planning is linked to portfolio projects and operational workload, and whether resource shortfalls are reported to management.","operating":"Compare the resource plan for the period against actual usage (e.g. time records); inspect the decisions taken for selected resource shortfalls.","evidence":["Resource capacity plan","Time tracking or resource utilization reports","Resource shortfall reports and related management decisions"],"findings":["The total workload of some individuals assigned to projects exceeds their capacity, but this was not visible during planning.","Staff in business units who perform IT tasks are not included in resource planning."],"iso":[],"clauses":["7.1"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"APO07.06","name":"Manage contract staff.","title":"Managing contract staff and consultants","expectation":"Consultants and contract staff should acknowledge the organization’s policies and confidentiality obligations before starting work, and contracts should include security requirements, intellectual property and performance expectations. Their access should be limited to the contract term, removed in a timely manner at contract end or upon a change of assignment, and organizational assets should be returned.","design":"Evaluate whether an inventory of contract staff is maintained, whether user accounts are tied to an end date and whether the contract end process triggers access removal.","operating":"Obtain the list of all external staff whose contracts ended during the period; for a risk-based selection, compare access removal dates against read-only system records, and check confidentiality undertakings for active contractors.","evidence":["Inventory of contract staff and consultants with contract terms","Signed confidentiality and policy acknowledgment forms","Account expiry dates and access removal records","Asset return records"],"findings":["The user accounts of some consultants whose contracts had ended remained active after the contract end date.","No signed confidentiality undertaking exists for some outsourced staff."],"iso":["5.20","6.2","6.5","6.6"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"access"}
]}
]
);
